Colonial Zong was the overseas colonial territory of the [[Zong|Kingdom of Zong]] on the western coast of [[Lurandia]], administered from [[Anping]] between 1474 and 1544. It was the predecessor of the [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]] and [[Louyang Colony|Louyang]] colonies, which were created by its division in 1544.

The territory was the second phase of Zong's colonial presence in the New World, following the failed settlement at [[Xinhai]] (1456). Unlike Xinhai, the colony at Anping succeeded, and it expanded along the western seaboard over the following six decades. Its history ended in the administrative division that produced the two colonies.

== Foundation ==

''See also: [[Anping]], [[Xinhai]]''

Zong's first permanent settlement in the New World was established at Xinhai in 1456, on the southern coast of the Lurandian mainland. It failed. The colony was founded in a region containing a large and well-organised indigenous population, and repeated attacks confined its settlers to a fortified harbour within two decades.

The second attempt was made with the lessons of the first. In 1474 the court authorised a settlement at Anping, a harbour site further north, selected during the exploratory voyages of the 1460s for its more favourable indigenous demography and its access to the riverine trade networks of the interior.

Anping succeeded where Xinhai had failed and became the seat of the Zong colonial government in the New World. It grew steadily, attracting merchants, farmers and artisans from the Zong mainland, and developed a plantation economy oriented toward tropical crops in demand in Fosian markets.

== Expansion ==

From Anping the colony expanded along the northwestern coast of Lurandia through the late fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries. Expeditions charted the coastline, and the court authorised a network of trading outposts and resupply stations.

The expansion was conducted under a policy that came to be articulated during this period: that the Zong presence was to be secured through a small number of defensible coastal positions rather than through the government of territory. This doctrine, described in the colonial correspondence as the holding of territorial islands, was the lesson drawn from the failure of Xinhai and was applied consistently thereafter.

The expansion was not without conflict. Indigenous communities resisted the Zong presence in some areas, and competition for trade access and territory produced periodic violence. The colonial response was generally a combination of military demonstration and diplomatic accommodation.

== Government ==

The colony was administered by a governor-general appointed by and reporting to the court in [[Zong]]. The office was responsible for the whole of the western Lurandian territory until the division of 1543.

Under the governor-general the colony was governed through a combination of crown appointees, merchant houses and the local colonial elite. The plantation districts of the south-west, centred on Anping, dominated the colony's economy and its administration. The newer settlements of the northern coast, including [[Tamsui]], founded in 1538, received comparatively little investment or attention.

The colony's military establishment consisted of a colonial garrison supplemented from the mid-sixteenth century by Sanese mercenaries, the [[Sea Wolves]], drawn from the dispossessed samurai of the [[Sengoku Period|Sengoku-era]] Sanese archipelago.

== Extent ==

[[File:Colonial Zong (1543).png|thumb|400px|The territory of Colonial Zong in 1543. The colony consisted of two separated coastal holdings: the settlements of the northwest, centred on Tamsui, and the older territory of the south-west, centred on Anping. Much of the coast between them remained uncolonised.]]
<!-- Image prompt: flat cropped map of western Lurandia showing Colonial Zong in dark green against grey uncolonised land and white ocean; two separated coastal holdings, no borders, no labels -->

The colony did not form a continuous territory. It consisted of two principal holdings separated by an extensive stretch of coast that the Zong had not colonised — the settlements of the northwest, centred on [[Tamsui]], and the older territory of the south-west, centred on [[Anping]].

This reflected the doctrine under which the colony was governed. The Zong presence was maintained through a small number of defensible coastal positions rather than through the government of territory, and no attempt was made to occupy or administer the coast between them. The map of the colony is accordingly a map of separated enclaves rather than of a continuous frontier.

The voyage between the northwestern settlements and Anping was long, and instructions issued at Anping took months to reach Tamsui. The delay shaped the character of the colony's government and contributed to the [[Tamsui Plot]] of 1543–1544.

== Economy and labour ==

The economy of Colonial Zong was built on plantation agriculture and the Shendan trade. Tropical crops — sugar, tobacco and others — were produced on estates worked initially by coerced indigenous labour and, increasingly, by enslaved people transported from elsewhere.

The enslavement of indigenous peoples was practised from the earliest years of the colony. The [[Zong Edict of 1500]] prohibited slavery on the Zong mainland but did not expressly extend to the overseas territories, creating an ambiguity that the court did not resolve. The colony consequently operated in a legal grey area in which slavery was banned in Zong itself and tolerated in the New World.

Labour shortages were persistent. The [[Secret Trade]] — the procurement of enslaved people from [[Dagit]] and the [[Balausia Peninsula]], conducted largely through piracy and kidnapping — developed in this period to supply the colonial plantations, and later supplied the Sinchew colony as well.

== First Mahik War ==

''See also: [[First Mahik War]]''

In 1538 the colonial administration concluded the [[Treaty of Anping]] with the [[Hachinawa|Hahkina state]] of the interior, recognising the Hahkina as a sovereign polity in exchange for a fixed coastal presence and a formal system of trade and arms supply. The treaty also provided for a joint campaign against the chiefdoms of the region north of the Hahkina.

The war was fought from 1538 to 1542 and ended with the partition of that region. The north, including the Tamsui harbour, passed to the colony; the south passed to the Hahkina. The acquisition of the northern coast gave the colony a second centre of settlement, and removed Tamsui's dependence on Anping for its supply and defence.

== Division ==

''See also: [[Tamsui Plot]]''

The acquisition of the coast did not resolve the administrative tension between the two settlements. In 1543 the colonial officials at Tamsui, supported by the settlement's merchant houses, refused to obey orders issued from Anping, producing several months of ineffective correspondence between the two administrations.

The dispute came before the [[Societal Assembly]] in Zong, which resolved it by dividing the colony. The territory northwest of the [[Qingjiang]] river, centred on Tamsui, became the [[Sinchew]] colony. The territory southeast of the river, centred on Anping, remained the [[Louyang]] colony. Each received its own governor and its own direct relationship with the court.

Colonial Zong thereby ceased to exist as a single jurisdiction, six decades after the founding of Anping and five years after the founding of Tamsui.

